# Mino installer — UI/UX generation prompt

Create a coherent set of high-fidelity UI mockups for Mino, an independent operating system with XP-era application compatibility. Focus on a guided installer, followed by a small desktop preview. This is a native operating system interface, not a website.

Use an original classic visual style: warm light-grey surfaces, restrained blue accents, crisp borders, simple original icons and readable sans-serif typography. No Microsoft logos, copied artwork or Luna assets. Avoid transparency, large animations, floating web cards and oversized marketing headings.

Design these five connected screens using one consistent layout:
1. Welcome: explain the goal and show a clear Start installation button. Include language selection only when supported.
2. Select disk: show identifiable disks with model, capacity and a visual partition map. Make the selected target unmistakable. Prioritize guided whole-disk installation; do not invent unsupported advanced controls.
3. Review: clearly show the selected disk and the before/after layout, identify which data will be removed, and name the EFI boot and system partitions in plain language. Keep Back available. Begin writing only after an explicit Install action.
4. Install: show real stages—prepare disk, create volumes, copy files, configure boot and verify. Use determinate progress only when measurable. Provide expandable details; explain cancellation availability honestly. Include a recoverable error state with Retry, details and a saved log.
5. Finish: show verified completion, a Restart into Mino action, and a failure state if boot configuration was not completed.

Target 1280×800, with a layout that also fits 800×600. Provide clear focus indicators, logical Tab order, Enter/Space activation, high-contrast text and status indicators that do not rely on colour alone. Use concise, calm language. Maintain consistent Back/Next/Install placement.

Show normal, focused, disabled, progress and error states. Add a short annotated flow explaining screen transitions and the point where disk writes begin. Include a matching desktop preview with taskbar, Start menu and file explorer. Do not imply networking, accounts, features or installation success that the implementation cannot provide.
